<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lift Every Voice and Sing (National Negro Anthem), Manhattan Harmony Four</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1923</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lift Every Voice and Sing, sculpture by Augusta Savage (retitled The Harp)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1939</span><span class="sg-chain-h">named after the song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings, Maya Angelou</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1969</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in the book</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Wattstax, film (Kim Weston)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1972</span><span class="sg-chain-h">performance in the film</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Melba Moore and others</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1990</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Voice of My Beautiful Country, René Marie</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2011</span><span class="sg-chain-h">its words sung to the melody of The Star-Spangled Banner</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Of Our New Day Begun, Omar Thomas</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2016</span><span class="sg-chain-h">concert band piece featuring the hymn</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Blues and Its People, Russell Gunn</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2023</span><span class="sg-chain-h">opening and closing song of the suite</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lift Every Voice: The Biden Plan for Black America</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2020</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title taken from the hymn</span></li></ul></section>
